From: Jeff Johnson <jjohnson@opsero.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 17:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] dmaengine: xilinx_dma: poll with a delay so the timeout is
 real time

All register polls in this driver go through xilinx_dma_poll_timeout(),
i.e. readl_poll_timeout_atomic(), with delay_us = 0 and
timeout_us = XILINX_DMA_LOOP_COUNT (1000000). read_poll_timeout_atomic()
does not use timekeeping: it subtracts delay_us * 1000 ns per iteration
when a delay is given, and otherwise only 1 ns per iteration. With a zero
delay the intended 1 s timeout therefore becomes 1e9 register reads.

On an AXI DMA whose S2MM channel is stopped mid-packet (the stream source
stalled with buffer descriptors still owned by the engine), DMASR.HALTED
never sets, and xilinx_dma_stop_transfer() spins for 1e9 AXI-Lite reads,
about 7 minutes on a ZynqMP, with preemption off. The CPU produces RCU
stall reports and the system is unresponsive until the poll finally gives
up ("Cannot stop channel ...: 10008") and xilinx_dma_chan_reset() recovers
the channel, which it does immediately.

Poll with a 1 us delay. The budget is then consumed in real time, so a
channel that cannot halt or reset is reported after about 1 s, as the
timeout intends. The delay only applies when the first read does not meet
the condition, so polls that complete immediately (the common case,
e.g. xilinx_dma_start() on every transfer start) are not slowed down.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Jeff Johnson <jjohnson@opsero.com>
---
 drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c | 15 +++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c b/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c
--- a/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c
@@ -165,6 +165,13 @@
 
 /* Delay loop counter to prevent hardware failure */
 #define XILINX_DMA_LOOP_COUNT		1000000
+/*
+ * Delay between register polls. readl_poll_timeout_atomic() does not use
+ * timekeeping: with a zero delay it decrements its nanosecond budget by one
+ * per iteration, so XILINX_DMA_LOOP_COUNT would allow 1e9 register reads
+ * (minutes on an AXI-Lite slave) instead of one second.
+ */
+#define XILINX_DMA_POLL_DELAY_US	1
 
 /* AXI DMA Specific Registers/Offsets */
 #define XILINX_DMA_REG_SRCDSTADDR	0x18
@@ -1312,7 +1319,7 @@
 
 	/* Wait for the hardware to halt */
 	return xilinx_dma_poll_timeout(chan, XILINX_DMA_REG_DMASR, val,
-				       val & XILINX_DMA_DMASR_HALTED, 0,
+				       val & XILINX_DMA_DMASR_HALTED, XILINX_DMA_POLL_DELAY_US,
 				       XILINX_DMA_LOOP_COUNT);
 }
 
@@ -1327,7 +1334,7 @@
 	u32 val;
 
 	return xilinx_dma_poll_timeout(chan, XILINX_DMA_REG_DMASR, val,
-				       val & XILINX_DMA_DMASR_IDLE, 0,
+				       val & XILINX_DMA_DMASR_IDLE, XILINX_DMA_POLL_DELAY_US,
 				       XILINX_DMA_LOOP_COUNT);
 }
 
@@ -1344,7 +1351,7 @@
 
 	/* Wait for the hardware to start */
 	err = xilinx_dma_poll_timeout(chan, XILINX_DMA_REG_DMASR, val,
-				      !(val & XILINX_DMA_DMASR_HALTED), 0,
+				      !(val & XILINX_DMA_DMASR_HALTED), XILINX_DMA_POLL_DELAY_US,
 				      XILINX_DMA_LOOP_COUNT);
 
 	if (err) {
@@ -1755,7 +1762,7 @@
 
 	/* Wait for the hardware to finish reset */
 	err = xilinx_dma_poll_timeout(chan, XILINX_DMA_REG_DMACR, tmp,
-				      !(tmp & XILINX_DMA_DMACR_RESET), 0,
+				      !(tmp & XILINX_DMA_DMACR_RESET), XILINX_DMA_POLL_DELAY_US,
 				      XILINX_DMA_LOOP_COUNT);
 
 	if (err) {
